Siderite Drive

Ingredients: INDUSTRIAL TECHNO & SCHRANZ + GARAGE ROCK + CELESTIAL, SPACE & ORBITAL + Double Bass  
Creative Catalyst: The scene bloomed in a condemned suburban shopping mall where runaway teenagers rewired abandoned arcade cabinets into fuzz preamps.

A concussive, high-velocity collision of blown-out garage rock fuzz, relentless 150-BPM industrial Schranz kick-drums, and the percussive slap of an acoustic upright double bass, all suspended inside the freezing, zero-gravity vacuum of massive orbital synthesizer reverbs.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 148–156 BPM. A manic, hard-driving four-on-the-floor kick pulse locked into the frantic, acoustic clatter of rockabilly-style upright double bass slapping, producing an unrelenting freight-train momentum.
- Harmonic Palette: Raw minor-pentatonic garage riffs and abrasive Locrian industrial drones colliding with wide, microtonal celestial chords (major sevenths with raised elevenths) voiced on tape-warped pads and bowed double bass overtones.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Chipped-DAC Saturation: Instrument signals are run through the converted digital-to-analog sound converters of salvaged 1990s arcade motherboards, yielding brittle, biting high-end aliasing.
  2. Re-Amped Acoustic Resonance: Upright double bass mic'd via internal piezos and played aggressively with a rosin-caked bow, blasted through a blown 100-watt tube amplifier cranked into full speaker breakup.
  3. Zero-G Sidechaining: Massive, cavernous celestial reverbs and modular synth sweeps violently ducked against clipped 909 kicks, creating an elastic breathing sensation between vacuum and impact.
- Dynamics: Extreme tension and explosive release. Tracks push listeners to the brink of sensory exhaustion through repetitive, stomping Schranz aggression before suddenly dropping out into weightless, floating orbital space drones, only to detonate back into full gear with a live drumstick count-in.

Scene & Origin Lore

Siderite Drive originated during the sub-zero winter of 2021 inside the Saint Jude Galleria, a condemned two-story retail complex outside Cleveland, Ohio. A transient collective of skate punks, art school dropouts, and warehouse techno purists breached the service tunnels and set up camp in "The Outer Limits," an abandoned coin-op arcade. Lacking conventional PA equipment, they soldered instrument jacks directly into the audio amps of gutted sit-down racing and space-combat cabinets, using the hollow fiberglass chassis as acoustic resonators. The fashion became an immediate reflection of their squat: grease-stained flight jumpsuits insulated with metallic survival blankets, heavy steel-toed work boots for dancing on broken glass, and homemade wire-mesh safety goggles. Parties were clandestine, unheated, and lit entirely by flickering CRT arcade monitors and blue emergency exit signs, where hundreds gathered to dance under frosted skylights while acoustic upright basses rattled the loose tiles of the dry central fountain.

Typical Instruments Used:
- Acoustic Upright Double Bass (outfitted with dual piezo pickups, gut strings, and played with both heavy percussive slap and aggressive bowing)
- Fuzz-drenched Garage Electric Guitars (Fender Mustang or Danelectro through modified vintage germanium fuzz pedals)
- Analog Drum Machines & Eurorack Schranz Modules (Roland TR-909 clones pushed into hard analog clipping)
- Salvaged Arcade Sound Chips (Yamaha YM2151 FM synthesizer boards repurposed as lead synths)
- Stereo Plate & Cosmic Reverb Units (Eventide Space, modular granular processors)

For Fans Of: A Place to Bury Strangers, Surgeon, The Horrors (Skying / Primary era), Blawan.

