Track 1: Tendrils Strangle the Subwoofer
- Story & Context: When seventeen-year-old Kaelen Vance first dragged his grandfather's splintered upright double bass into the Saint Jude Galleria, the dead air smelled of scorched solder and stale orange soda. The signature sound was born entirely by accident when security guards raided the arcade and guitarist Clara "Rook" Min tripped over a power strip, violently jamming an unshielded patch cable into an overclocked Galaga audio board that sent an apocalyptic 152-BPM square wave shrieking across the linoleum. Before the flashlights could corner them, Rook grabbed a dented dynamic microphone, shouted "spit the copper out and let the root take hold," and locked Vance's frantic acoustic bass slap directly into the machine's redlining distortion loop.
